WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.313
This video uses the Human Reads demo project.

00:00:06.313 --> 00:00:11.847
Choose Help, then Demo Projects, and click Download and Open beside Human Reads.

00:00:11.847 --> 00:00:14.959
The video Your first project shows each step.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:19.029
Click the HG002 reads under Imports.

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:22.081
HG002 is a well studied human sample.

00:00:22.081 --> 00:00:32.640
The cards across the top sum up all the reads in this file, how many there are, how long they are, and how good their bases are.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:39.684
Each base gets a quality score, the sequencer's own estimate of error, written Q.

00:00:39.684 --> 00:00:42.473
Q20 means one wrong base in a hundred.

00:00:42.473 --> 00:00:44.528
Q30 means one in a thousand.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.208
Here 91 percent of bases reach Q30.

00:00:48.208 --> 00:00:55.499
For Illumina, the most common short read sequencer, a rule of thumb calls above 80 percent healthy.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.329
Click the Q / Position chart to open it.

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:05.266
Quality falls toward the end of each read, which is normal for this kind of sequencing.

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:06.904
Here it stays above Q20.

00:01:06.904 --> 00:01:09.360
Click outside the chart to close it.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.430
Length Dist.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.877
peaks at 250 bases, the length the run was set to make.

00:01:15.877 --> 00:01:24.124
GC content, the share of bases that are G or C, is 39 percent, close to the human average of about 41.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:31.285
Next, see these reads lined up against the human genome, on the Videos page of the website.
